Planned scope
The guide will document a reproducible, version-aware approach to SOMAmer filtering for cerebrospinal fluid analyses. It will distinguish assay-level quality control from study-specific filtering and provide a reason for every analyte retained or excluded.
Planned topics include:

the assay version and stable SOMAmer identifiers used for matching;
matrix suitability and detectability in CSF;
technical reproducibility and missing or flagged measurements;
evidence relevant to specificity, cross-reactivity, or off-target binding;
sensitivity to blood contamination or other pre-analytical factors;
treatment of duplicate or multiple reagents targeting the same protein;
sensitivity analyses using broader and narrower SOMAmer sets; and
an exportable inclusion table with one row per SOMAmer and explicit reason codes.

What to balance
Start with the variables required by the primary analysis. Depending on the study, these may include:

primary group, treatment arm, or phenotype;
collection site or cohort;
sex, age band, ancestry, or another major biological stratum;
visit or time point, while keeping repeated samples from the same participant linked appropriately for the study objective;
collection date, freezer location, extraction batch, or other known handling factors; and
technical replicates or bridge samples, when used.

Not every variable can be perfectly balanced. Prioritize the primary contrast, avoid empty group-by-plate combinations, and involve the statistician and assay laboratory before the final map is locked.
